const proseTitle = 'Lofi is a set of deliberate imperfections';
const proseBody = `
  <p>Lofi is not a tempo or a chord progression. It is detuned keys, drums that drag slightly behind the grid, tape hiss, and a lowpass filter where a mix engineer would put clarity. The genre grew out of producers like J Dilla and Nujabes sampling jazz records and leaving the dust in, and the modern lofi hip hop playlist economy still runs on that same aesthetic: warm, loose, and unpolished on purpose. The problem for anyone making it today is sourcing. Sampling old records cannot be released without a clearance, and sample packs hand the same Rhodes loop to thousands of other producers.</p>
  <p>Sonura generates the ingredients instead. Describe the chords and the room, and the <strong>drums</strong>, <strong>bass</strong> and <strong>melody</strong> come back as <strong>separate stems</strong> you can swing, filter and saturate yourself. That matters more in lofi than in most genres, because the processing is the genre. A finished stereo loop has its swing, its filter and its crackle fixed. Split stems let you push the hats late, roll the keys off at 8 kHz, sidechain the bass to the kick, and drop the texture out for the bridge so it lands harder when it returns. Learn more about <a href="/features/stem-export/">stem export</a>, or start with the free <a href="/tools/ai-beat-maker/">beat maker</a>.</p>
  <p>The sounds themselves follow the lofi formula: a soft round kick, a rimshot or snare with almost no top end, hats that sound like they were recorded in the next room, and Rhodes or soft piano playing 7ths and 9ths in close voicings. The models are trained on ethically licensed data, so the question of whether you can release the result is answered before you start. Need a full arrangement with a vocal on top? Use the <a href="/tools/ai-song-maker/">AI song maker</a>. Want harder drums under a rapper? Switch to the <a href="/genre/hip-hop/">rap beat maker</a>.</p>
`;

